  "headline": "Decisive Abroad, Undone at Home",
  "regional_lead": "Western Europe's governments are at their most decisive abroad and most fragile at home — and the gap is not incidental. In Britain, France, and Italy, the most consequential decisions this week were made in planning rooms and palace corridors, well away from parliaments that are hostile, paralysed, or not yet asked. The commitments being made will outlast the domestic authority of the governments making them, and the governments know it.\n\nBritain is the clearest case. Labour lost over 1,100 council seats across England on May 7-8, ceded the Welsh Senedd, and failed to dislodge the Scottish National Party, which won a fifth consecutive Scottish parliamentary term. By the weekend, roughly 40 Labour MPs were calling for Keir Starmer to quit; Catherine West had set a Monday deadline for a departure timetable; Angela Rayner declared that \"what we are doing isn't working.\" Yet while Westminster consumed itself, John Healey, the defence secretary, and Air Chief Marshal Richard Knighton approved the redeployment of HMS Dragon — the UK's most capable air-defence platform — from Cyprus to the Persian Gulf, and Britain and France scheduled a meeting of over 40 defence ministers for May 12 to finalise operational plans for the Hormuz coalition they are jointly leading. Mr Starmer's answer to the election was to frame closer EU ties as his political response — but a diplomatic commitment's credibility depends on the survival of the government making it. Foreign governments negotiating with a prime minister facing a live leadership challenge will factor that in.\n\nFrance and Italy are on opposite sides of the same pressure. France has already committed. The week's most consequential revelation was buried in a dispatch on the Charles de Gaulle's Suez Canal transit: Rafale fighters have been shooting down Iranian drones and missiles over the United Arab Emirates since February. France is already at war in the Gulf, just without saying so. Emmanuel Macron then retreated — insisting France never intended a unilateral mission, phoning Iran's president to say so — while Tehran responded with a threat of immediate retaliation regardless. Italy has not yet committed, and that is precisely the problem. Rome's position — minesweepers only after a ceasefire and parliamentary authorisation — is being pressed from two directions: Washington wants action against Iran now; Paris and London want Italian ships moving before any ceasefire, so they are in position when one comes. Marco Rubio visited Rome, produced something called the \"Rome Coalition,\" and the following morning the American president told Corriere della Sera that Italy \"wasn't there when we needed them.\" Giorgia Meloni was meanwhile reinforcing her European ties — meeting Volodymyr Zelensky in Yerevan, receiving Libya's prime minister in Rome, flying to Azerbaijan to shore up gas supply through the Trans Adriatic Pipeline. The insurance policy is being built even as the main relationship strains.\n\nSpain shows what happens when foreign policy becomes an instrument of domestic survival. Ten days before Andalusia votes, Pedro Sánchez awarded Spain's civil merit decoration to Francesca Albanese, the UN special rapporteur on Palestine, in a ceremony at the Moncloa staged to be seen. On the same day, the foreign minister appeared before the full Congress to explain Spain's protection of a Gaza-bound flotilla. These are not reactive moves; they are deliberate escalations timed for an election campaign — sovereign acts of foreign confrontation used to mobilise a base that domestic politics has failed to hold. Germany offers the opposite failure: a government that cannot execute even routine policy at home. Lars Klingbeil, the finance minister, was flying to Toronto to make Germany's case for global economic partnership when he learned the Bundesrat had killed the government's flagship energy relief measure — defeated not by the opposition but by SPD-governed states, because Berlin had kept the tobacco tax revenue and left the states to bear nearly two-thirds of the €2.8 billion cost.\n\nItaly's parliamentary hearing on Hormuz on May 13 will be the first real test of whether Rome can commit to its European partners while keeping its coalition together. Spain's Andalusia vote on May 17 will show whether foreign-policy confrontation buys domestic survival or merely postpones a harder reckoning. In Britain, gilt markets have not panicked; the FTSE 250 rose despite the political turbulence, and markets appear to be pricing a leadership transition as neutral or even positive — suggesting the crisis facing Mr Starmer is one of political legitimacy, not economic collapse. That distinction matters, because the Hormuz coalition Britain is co-leading will need a government capable of making binding decisions for at least two years. The commitments made this week in planning rooms and palace corridors will outlast the governments that made them. That is not strength. It is the week's central risk.",
  "gap_paragraphs": [
    "Notably absent this week: NATO institutional coherence signal — with Germany pursuing Tomahawk procurement, France framing its Hormuz mission as distinct from US Project Freedom, UK building a 40-nation coalition outside NATO architecture, and Italy holding a parliamentary authorization condition, the regional framework predicts that NATO's role in the Hormuz theater would be an active topic of alliance management, producing visible NATO-level signaling or friction. The absence of NATO from the Hormuz coalition-building dynamic is analytically significant. The UK-France co-leadership of the 40-nation coalition using Lancaster House rather than NATO architecture reflects a structural choice — or an implicit acknowledgment that NATO consensus on Iran conflict engagement is not achievable. The gap could reflect: (1) a genuine institutional bypass, where European powers have concluded NATO cannot produce Hormuz consensus and are building parallel architecture; (2) a collection gap, where NATO-level discussions are occurring but below the media threshold that drives country desk reporting; or (3) deliberate signaling that the Hormuz coalition is not a war-entry mechanism (consistent with Italy's ceasefire condition and France's 'distinct from US' framing). The gap warrants explicit monitoring in the next cycle.",
    "Notably absent this week: Spain-Italy Mediterranean security coordination — both countries are active in the Hormuz-adjacent security environment (Italy pressing toward naval deployment, Spain hosting CHENS-26 with 35 European navy chiefs), and the regional framework predicts bilateral Mediterranean security dialogue between the two countries that share both southern-flank exposure and North African energy dependencies. This could reflect genuine absence of coordination (the two countries are operating on different strategic tracks — Italy toward Hormuz deployment decision, Spain toward domestic election), a collection gap (bilateral security coordination below media threshold), or the structural reality that Spain has not yet been pulled into the Hormuz coalition pressure that is bearing on Italy. If CHENS-26 produced any Spain-Italy naval coordination signals, they are not captured in either desk's reporting. The gap is a watch indicator: if Spain is eventually approached to join the Hormuz coalition framework, the Spain-Italy bilateral relationship will become relevant."
  ],
  "card_summary": "Europe is most decisive abroad at the moment it is most fragile at home."
}
